course: dictionary
lesson: 3219
title: Enrobe: dressed or coated
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Enrobe is a rare, formal word, so you may not know it yet. Its clues are in the words around it. Enrobe can mean to dress someone in a robe, or to coat food all over, especially in chocolate.
explain: Read this part of a factory sentence: «...passing them on a wire belt through a falling curtain of melted chocolate, with a pool below for the bottoms; this is called enrobing.» The bars go through melted chocolate, so enrobing must mean coating them. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Coat or cover would fit, so enrobe is close to coat.
explain: On a sweet packet, you might read «enrobed in milk chocolate». The sweet is covered in chocolate. In old or ceremonial writing, enrobed can mean a judge or bishop dressed in special robes. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
fact: Enrobe is a verb: enrobe, enrobes, enrobed, enrobing.
fact: In a formal sense, «enrobe» means to dress someone in a robe or special clothes.
fact: In cooking, «enrobe» means to coat food all over, especially in chocolate.
fact: «Enrobing» is the name for the process of coating food, as in «this is called enrobing».
fact: On a sweet packet, «enrobed in milk chocolate» means covered all over in milk chocolate.
exercise: «The sweets were enrobed in milk chocolate.» What does enrobed mean here? | Frozen solid | *Coated all over | Thrown into a box | Shared out fairly
exercise: «The judge was enrobed for the ceremony.» What does enrobed mean? | Late for the court | Sent home early | *Dressed in a special robe
exercise: In the factory, what does enrobing do to the bars? | Cuts them into pieces | *Coats them in chocolate | Lays caramel out as a sheet | Sends them to a pool
test: «The bishop was enrobed in purple robes for the service.» What does enrobed mean? | Hid from the crowd | Went to bed | Painted purple | *Dressed in special clothes
test: Which sentence uses enrobe in the cooking sense? | The bird enrobed its nest in moss | *The baker enrobed the truffles in dark chocolate | The knight enrobed his horse in armour | The king enrobed his son in gold
exam: What does «enrobed in milk chocolate» on a sweet packet tell you? | The sweet is shaped like a robe | The sweet is made of milk | *The sweet is covered all over in milk chocolate | The sweet is wrapped in paper
